<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Melingoi</h1><p class="st-meta">700 CE – 1300 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Slavic tribe of the Taygetus mountains in the Peloponnese that kept a semi-independent life under Byzantine and Frankish rule for centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Melingoi were descendants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> who settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> in the late 6th and 7th centuries. By the 9th century they lived on the slopes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-taygetus">Mount Taygetus</a>, west of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sparta">Sparta</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-administrando-imperio">De Administrando Imperio</a> describes their revolt and subjugation by the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theoktistos-bryennios">Theoktistos Bryennios</a> in the 840s.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Living in mountain villages under their own leaders, the Melingoi paid the Byzantine state a small fixed tribute, 60 gold coins a year according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a>, rather than ordinary taxes. They revolted again in the 920s and had their tribute raised before it was reduced once more. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-the-morea">Chronicle of the Morea</a> shows them still distinct in the 13th century.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the Frankish conquest, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-villehardouin">William of Villehardouin</a> built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mystras">Mistras</a> and other castles in 1249 partly to control them; they later served as soldiers for both <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/franks">Franks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>. Gradually Christianised and Hellenised, they disappear as a separate group by the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/melingoi.jpg" alt="Melingoi" width="320" height="251"><figcaption>Image: Eric Gaba (Sting - fr:Sting); derivative work by Constantine · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peloponnese_Middle_Ages_map-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>700 CE – 1300 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Slavic paganism, later Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Slavic, later Medieval Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Milingoi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1000&amp;focus=melingoi" aria-label="Melingoi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5210 750 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Melingoi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0M5530 1071h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5530 1006h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5530 1006h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Greece. <a href="/atlas?year=1000&amp;focus=melingoi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

